  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on an aspiration level (3000) and prefers the lottery with the higher chance of exceeding it. Lottery B has a 0.1% chance of exceeding 3000 (6000 > 3000), while Lottery A has no outcome exceeding 3000 (3000 is not greater than 3000), so the reason would pick B. The individual does not mention any aspiration level or comparison of outcomes to a reference point. Instead, they explicitly mention using expected value and, when that is tied, choosing based on the higher possible reward under low probability (i.e., 'higher win if lucky'). This is a different rationale: it is outcome maximization under low probability rather than aspiration-level comparison. Although both the reason and the individual end up favoring Lottery B, the justification is structurally different. The individual does not reference deviations from a mean outcome or evaluate probabilities of exceeding a computed aspiration level. Therefore, the cognitive process described does not align with the decision reason. However, there is partial overlap in considering all outcomes and focusing on higher payoffs, which may suggest superficial similarity. But the core mechanism (aspiration level + comparison of probabilities of positive deviations) is absent.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

